power systems use lenses or mirrors and solar tracking systems to focus a large area of sunlight to a hot spot, often to drive a steam turbine. Photovoltaics (PV) were initially solely used as a source of electricity for small and medium-sized applications, from the calculator powered by a single solar cell to remote homes powered by an off-grid rooftop PV system. Commercial concentrated solar power plants were first developed in the 1980s. Since then, as the cost of solar panels has fallen, grid-connected solar PV systems' capacity and production have doubled about every three years. Three-quarters of new generation capacity is solar,[2] with both millions of rooftop installations and gigawatt-scale photovoltaic power stations continuing to be built. In 2025, solar power generated 9% of global electricity.[3] In 2024, solar generated 7% of global electricity and over 1% of primary energy (2.7% by the substitution method), adding twice as much new electricity as coal.[4][5] Along with onshore wind power, utility-scale solar is the source with the cheapest levelised cost of electricity for new installations in most countries.[6][7] Almost half the solar power installed in 2022 was mounted on rooftops.[8] China is currently the largest producer and installer of solar power capacity; globally, it produces 98% of solar wafers, 92% of solar cells and 85% of solar panels, and accounted for more than 55% of global installed solar capacity in the first half of 2025.[9][10][11] Much more low-carbon power is needed for electrification and to limit climate change.[2] The International Energy Agency said in 2022 that more effort was needed for grid integration and the mitigation of policy, regulation and financing challenges.[12] Nevertheless solar may greatly cut the cost of energy.[4] Solar is important for energy security.[13] Potential[edit] Part of a series onSustainable energy showEnergy conservation Arcology Building insulation Cogeneration Compact fluorescent lamp Eco h